<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-fiber-product" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Pullback/Fiber product</title>
  <p>
    In this section we describe the <em>pullback</em> (also called <em>fiber product</em>) construction for continuous functions on topological spaces.
  </p>
  <definition xml:id="def-pullback-top-spaces">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Let <m>X,Y</m> and <m>Z</m> be topological spaces. Suppose that <m>f\colon X\to Z</m> and <m>g\colon Y\to Z</m> be continuous maps. The <em>pullback</em> of <m>X,Y</m> along continuous functions <m>f,g</m> is a topological space <m>X\times_ZY</m> together with continuous functions <m>\pi_X\colon X\times_ZY\to X</m> and <m>\pi_Y\colon X\times_ZY\to Y</m> such that <m>f\circ\pi_X=g\circ\pi_Y</m> and that satisfy the following <em>universal property</em>.
        </p>
        <p>
          If <m>W</m> is any topological space with continuous maps <m>p_X\colon W\to X</m> and <m>p_Y\colon W\to Y</m> such that <m>f\circ p_X=g\circ p_Y</m>, then there exists a unique continuous map <m>h\colon W\to X\times_ZY</m> such that <m>p_X=\pi_X\circ X</m> and <m>p_Y=\pi_Y\circ h</m>.
        </p>
        <figure>
          <caption>Universal property of pullback</caption>
          <image width="50%">
          <latex-image>
            
\begin{tikzcd}
W \arrow[rdd, bend right,"p_X",labels=below] \arrow[rrd, bend left,"p_Y"] \arrow[rd, dotted,"h"] \amp                                \amp             \\
                                                                    \amp X\times_ZY \arrow[d,"\pi_X"] \arrow[r,"\pi_Y"] \amp Y \arrow[d,"g"] \\
                                                                    \amp X \arrow[r,"f"]                    \amp Z          
\end{tikzcd}
          </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <remark xml:id="existence-of-pullback">
    <statement>
      <p>
        We keep notations of <xref ref="def-pullback-top-spaces"/>. Consider the following subset of <m>X\times Y</m> with the subspace topology.
        <me>
          X\times_ZY=\left\{(x,y)\in X\times Y:f(x)=g(y)\right\}
        </me>
        It can be verified that the above subspace together with natural projection <m>\pi_X\colon X\times_ZY\to X</m> (resp., <m>\pi_Y\colon X\times_ZY\to Y</m>) given by <m>\pi_X((x,y))=x</m> (resp., given by <m>\pi_Y((x,y))=y</m>) is pullback.
      </p>
      <p>
        This shows existence of pullback.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </remark>
  <remark xml:id="uniqueness-of-pullback">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The pullback given in <xref ref="existence-of-pullback"/> is unique up to homeomorphism. 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </remark>
  <example>
  <title>(Fiber over a point as a pullback)</title>
  
  
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>X,Y</m> be a topological spaces and let <m>Z=\{\star\}</m> be a one point set.
      </p>
      <p>
        Let <m>f\colon X\to\{\star\}</m> and <m>g\colon Y\to\{\star\}</m> be unique continuous maps. In this case we get that <m>X\times_ZY</m> is homeomorphic to <m>X\times Y</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Suppose that <m>h\colon X\to Y</m> is a continuous map. Let <m>g'\colon\{\star\}\to Y</m> be a map that maps <m>g'(\star)=y_0</m> for some <m>y_0\in Y</m>. We get that <m>X\times_YZ</m> is homeomorphic to <m>h^{-1}(y_0)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>
  
  <example>
  <title>(Intersection as pullback)</title>
  
  
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose that <m>Z</m> is a topological space and that <m>X,Y</m> be subspaces of <m>Z</m>. Consider the canonical inclusions, viz., <m>i_X\colon X\hookrightarrow Z</m> and <m>i_Y\colon Y\hookrightarrow Z</m>. The pullback of <m>X, Y</m> along these inclusions <m>i_X,i_Y</m> is the following.
        <md>
          <mrow>X\times_ZY \amp=\left\{(x,y)\in X\times Y:i_X(x)=i_Y(y)\right\} </mrow>
          <mrow> \amp=\left\{(x,y)\in X\times Y:x=y\right\} </mrow>
          <mrow> \amp\simeq X\cap Y </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <example>
  <title>(Pullback as graph of a function)</title>
  
  
    <statement>
      <p>
        The continuous functions involved in the pullback construction are important. For instance the pullback of <m>\R</m> along identity functions is <m>\R\times_\R\R=\{(x,x)\in\R\times\R\}\simeq\R</m>. However, the pullback of <m>\R</m> along <m>f\colon\R\to\R</m> given by <m>x\mapsto x^2</m> along with the identity is <m>\R\times_\R\R=\{(x,y)\in\R\times\R:x^2=y\}</m>. Observe that, in this case, the pullback is the graph of <m>y=x^2</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>
</section>